(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which epidermolysis bullosa subtype carries the highest mortality?","choices":["Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, especially the localised palmoplantar form","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, especially the dominant form","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, the autoimmune acquired form","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, especially the Herlitz subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why early palliative care integration is appropriate in severe Herlitz disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which androgen is chiefly responsible for male-pattern hair loss?","choices":["Dihydrotestosterone","Testosterone acting directly on the follicle","Oestradiol","Cortisol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — male-pattern hair loss is an androgen-dependent trait with a strong paternal influence on risk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a barrier to treatment and medication adherence?","choices":["Ready access to transportation","Cost and affordability of medications or services","High health literacy","Strong communication and follow-up by the care team"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cost and affordability heads the list of barriers carried over from Patient-Centered Healthcare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which three cue sources feed the start of the hypothetico-deductive sequence?","choices":["Ask, advise, and assess","Published guidelines, a literature review, and the cost data for each test","Risk, benefit, and likelihood of success","Initial history, initial examination, and screening diagnostic testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with initial history cues, initial examination cues and screening diagnostic testing cues. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Where is nevus simplex most common, and what makes it more noticeable?","choices":["Head and neck; crying","Trunk; exposure to sunlight","Extremities; cold temperature","Face and upper trunk; pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pink to erythematous blanchable macules or patches, single or multiple. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What distinguishes an ulceration from an erosion?","choices":["An ulceration involves only the epidermis and always heals without scarring, while an erosion reaches the dermis or deeper","An ulceration is full-thickness loss extending to the dermis or deeper; an erosion loses only the epidermis","An ulceration is linear and an erosion is round","An ulceration is painful and an erosion is painless"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — depth is the discriminator, and it determines whether the lesion can scar. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which conditions are named in the differential for scabies?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis, body and pubic lice, other arthropod bites, dermatitis herpetiformis, fungal infection and psoriasis","Cellulitis, erysipelas, necrotizing fasciitis and a deep vein thrombosis of the whole affected lower leg","Lentigo maligna, seborrheic keratosis, café-au-lait macule and freckles","Bullous pemphigoid, pemphigus, thermal burn and herpes zoster infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — all can produce an intensely itchy eruption. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the dimple sign, and which lesion shows it?","choices":["The lesion blanches completely under direct downward pressure; cherry angioma","The lesion transmits a wave-like fluid shift on palpation; pilonidal abscess","The lesion expresses pasty material through a central pore; epidermoid cyst","The lesion retracts beneath the skin surface with lateral compression; dermatofibroma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — this is the physical sign the lecture asks about directly. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has vitiligo affecting roughly 20% of his body surface. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Phototherapy with narrow band ultraviolet B, ideally combined with topical therapy","Topical corticosteroid alone applied twice daily to all the affected areas","Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented areas","PUVA phototherapy, which is preferred to narrow band ultraviolet B here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over PUVA, which raises skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which red flags does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, ulceration, induration, fixation, or palpable regional nodes","Slow growth, painlessness, a stable size, soft consistency, free mobility, and no palpable nodes","Pruritus, fine scaling, symmetry of the lesion, sharp demarcation, and a waxy stuck-on surface","Sandpaper texture, tenderness on palpation, flesh-coloured hue, and a size under 6 mm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — seven features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A child has genital molluscum lesions. What does the deck say about interpreting this?","choices":["It confirms sexual abuse, so a report should be filed on the location alone","It excludes sexual abuse, because genital lesions in children are always autoinoculated","It requires no assessment at all, because genital lesions are expected in children","It requires context-sensitive assessment, because location alone does not prove abuse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — location alone is not proof. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with candidal intertrigo is prescribed a topical azole. Beyond the antifungal, which environmental measures does the lecture require?","choices":["Expose the folds to direct sunlight daily, avoid moisture-wicking fabric, and treat incontinence only if it is severe","Gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis","Apply an occlusive dressing to the folds, avoid all washing and drying of the area, use a non-absorbent fabric, and treat incontinence only once the rash has settled","Soak the folds in warm water twice daily, apply talcum powder, and cover the area with a waterproof film"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — correcting the environment is listed first. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"That infant is to be treated medically. Which is the first-line option?","choices":["Intralesional methotrexate","A beta-blocker","Intralesional fluorouracil","Oral isotretinoin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — oral propranolol or topical timolol, whose mechanism is not well understood. Corticosteroids are the other named first-line treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How is a poorly demarcated lesion described?","choices":["Raised above the surrounding skin surface","Irregular or blotchy in appearance, without well-defined borders","Smaller than one centimetre in every dimension measured","Present in more than one body region"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — as against a well demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 24-year-old has a first episode of painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base on the vulva, which have broken down into shallow ulcers. She has fever, headache and tender inguinal nodes. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary syphilis","Chancroid ulceration","Vulvovaginal candidiasis","Herpes simplex virus infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — grouped vesicles becoming shallow painful ulcers, with a systemic prodrome. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which points should the conversation with a hyperhidrosis patient cover?","choices":["That a single course of treatment produces a permanent cure","That the condition is rare and few treatment options exist","That it is not about poor hygiene or anxiety, with moisture-wicking clothing suggested and realistic expectations set","That improved washing and a reduction in stress will resolve all of the sweating within a few months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — botulinum toxin needs repeating every three to six months and can be cost-prohibitive. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 41-year-old describes severe stabbing pain under one fingernail, worse in cold water, with an exquisitely tender point. The nail itself looks almost normal, with a small red-blue spot beneath it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Glomus tumour","Nail unit melanoma","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma","Subungual haematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the triad of paroxysmal pain, point tenderness and cold sensitivity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Head lice have recurred after two pediculicide courses. Which approach is described?","choices":["A multimodal approach combining physical methods with adjuvant chemical therapy","A third course of the same pediculicide at double the concentration","Occlusive home remedies such as mayonnaise or mineral oil alone","Oral ivermectin given as a single dose to the whole household"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — increasing resistance is the stated reason. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"At what Breslow thickness is sentinel lymph node biopsy offered or discussed, and what lowers that threshold?","choices":["At 4.0 mm or greater; or at 2.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration or high mitotic rate","At any thickness once the diagnosis is confirmed, since thickness does not influence the decision to stage the nodes","At 1.0 mm or greater; or at 0.8 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion","At 0.8 mm or greater; or at 1.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three risk factors lower the threshold. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which re-excision margins does the deck give for definitive local treatment of melanoma?","choices":["In situ 2 cm; less than 1 mm thick, a 3 cm margin; more than 1 mm thick, a 5 cm margin","In situ 0.5 to 1 cm; less than 1 mm thick, a 2 cm margin; more than 1 mm thick, a 0.5 cm margin","A uniform 1 cm margin for every melanoma regardless of thickness or in situ status","In situ 0.5 to 1 cm; less than 1 mm thick, a 1 cm margin; more than 1 mm thick, a 1 to 2 cm margin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — margin scales with Breslow thickness. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has an eruption whose reaction pattern has been recognised. Which characteristics are used next to narrow the differential?","choices":["Culture and sensitivity of the lesion together with a complete blood count and chemistry","Presence of pruritus, presence of pain, and whether regional lymph nodes are enlarged","Colour, shape, configuration of the lesions relative to one another, and their distribution on the body","Duration of the eruption, the patient's age, and whether any treatment has been tried so far"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those tune the differential the reaction pattern has already generated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How soon after the injury does each of these scars appear, and what happens with time?","choices":["Both develop within four weeks of the injury and both flatten over the following year","Both develop months after the trauma and neither improves without treatment","Hypertrophic scars develop soon after surgery and usually improve with time; keloids may develop months after the trauma and rarely improve","Hypertrophic scars develop months after the trauma and rarely improve; keloids develop soon after surgery and usually improve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — timing and trajectory both separate them. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 47-year-old healthy woman has a small area of cellulitis on the forearm with minimal pain, no fever and no risk factors for serious illness. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Plain films of the forearm, since underlying osteomyelitis must be excluded","Venous duplex ultrasound, since deep vein thrombosis must be excluded first","No workup, since the diagnosis is clinical in limited disease without systemic signs","Blood cultures and a punch biopsy, since organism identification guides therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serious infection warrants blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 78-year-old has a slowly enlarging irregular brown patch on the cheek, present for years, on skin that is heavily sun-damaged. Which melanoma subtype does this fit?","choices":["Lentigo maligna","Superficial spreading","Nodular","Acral lentiginous"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults, slow radial growth. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A patient with a few small asymptomatic common warts asks for aggressive freezing to remove them quickly. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Aggressive freezing is appropriate, because common warts do not resolve without treatment","Avoid freezing entirely, because cryotherapy is never an appropriate wart treatment","Avoid excessive freezing or destructive therapy for benign lesions likely to resolve spontaneously","Aggressive freezing is appropriate, because it eradicates the virus and prevents recurrence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — weigh the harms against a self-limiting lesion. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a sudden shower of small raindrop-shaped scaly pink papules over the trunk and proximal limbs. He had a sore throat about two weeks ago. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pityriasis rosea","Plaque psoriasis","Nummular eczema","Guttate psoriasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — raindrop-shaped papules in a child 2 to 3 weeks after streptococcal infection or an upper respiratory infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"What is the relationship between naevus number and melanoma in dysplastic naevus syndrome?","choices":["Naevus number has no bearing at all on melanoma risk","Only the single largest naevus determines the overall risk","The higher the number of naevi, the higher the risk of melanoma","The higher the number of naevi, the lower the risk of melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — over one hundred by adolescence defines the syndrome. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the earliest sign of an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["A pink to erythematous irregular macule that becomes more noticeable when the baby cries","A bright red exophytic papule with a moist surface that bleeds after minor trauma","Blanching of the involved skin, then fine telangiectasias, then a red or crimson macule","A flat well-circumscribed blanchable patch that darkens with crying, fever or overheating"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — about half are present at birth as a prominent cutaneous mark. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A patient elects to have a benign lesion removed purely for appearance. What must she be told first?","choices":["That lifelong surveillance of the site will be needed","The risk of pigmentary changes and the chance of recurrence","That histology is required on every excised specimen","That the lesion would become malignant if left in place"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both must be fully explained beforehand. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks what her lesions mean for her health. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesions themselves are benign, but they mark sun damage that is also associated with actinic keratosis and skin cancers","The lesions are premalignant and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a melanoma","The lesions indicate an underlying internal malignancy and warrant an urgent systemic screening of the whole body","The lesions will fade over the coming winter if she avoids further exposure to the sun"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the association includes squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has early Lyme disease with erythema migrans. Which is the most appropriate oral antibiotic?","choices":["Doxycycline","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone","Amoxicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is the alternative first line for early disease in children and pregnant patients. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has hundreds of small papules over the trunk and limbs, present for a year and mildly itchy. Biopsy confirms granuloma annulare. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Screen for diabetes, thyroid disease and dyslipidaemia, with age-appropriate malignancy screening","Reassure that the condition is benign and arrange review in two years","Begin intralesional triamcinolone to each of the affected lesions","Begin oral terbinafine on the assumption of a widespread dermatophyte infection of the trunk and limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — generalised disease in an adult over fifty carries these associations, including lymphoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with thick silvery scale over both elbows, knees and the sacrum. Scraping the scale produces pinpoint bleeding. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Lichen simplex chronicus","Plaque psoriasis","Nummular eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The extensor distribution with silvery scale and the Auspitz sign (bleeding on scale removal) are classic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has honey-coloured crusted lesions around the nose and mouth that appeared four days after a scratch, and tender submandibular nodes. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ecthyma of the face and perioral area","Non-bullous impetigo","Bullous impetigo","Herpes simplex of the perioral skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a honey-coloured adherent crust on the face with regional lymphadenopathy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has generalised darkly pigmented lentigines over much of his body. Which additional consideration does this raise?","choices":["An internal malignancy should be considered, and urgent screening arranged","An inherited disorder should be considered, as with LAMB and myxoma syndrome","Neurocutaneous melanosis should be considered, and brain imaging arranged","Dysplastic naevus syndrome should be considered, and every lesion biopsied"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a partial or generalised lentigo is what prompts that question. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does pulsed dye laser do to a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["Reduces scar erythema by reducing neovascularization","Reduces scar height by cross-linking collagen fibers","Reduces pruritus by destroying cutaneous nerve endings","Reduces recurrence by removing the entire cyst capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it also decreases scar height and improves texture. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"When should resistant dermatophytosis be suspected?","choices":["Only in immunocompromised patients","Widespread, intensely inflammatory, linked, or terbinafine-refractory","When the lesion clears centrally within two weeks of starting treatment","Whenever a potassium hydroxide preparation is negative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — epidemiologically linked cases and failure of an adequate terbinafine course both count. Obtain species identification and susceptibility testing when available. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with pseudofolliculitis barbae must remain clean-shaven for work. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Use a single or double blade at a mild angle, avoid lift-and-cut systems, and keep razors clean","Shave daily against the direction of growth so the hairs are cut below the skin surface","Use the closest-cutting multi-blade razor available to reduce the frequency of shaving","Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical corticosteroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with chemical depilatories and laser hair removal as alternatives. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do primary and secondary skin lesions differ in the depth they affect?","choices":["Primary lesions appear first in time while secondary lesions appear only after treatment has been started","Primary lesions affect the epidermis and superficial dermis; secondary lesions infiltrate the dermis or subcutaneous tissue","Primary lesions infiltrate the dermis and subcutaneous tissue; secondary lesions affect only the superficial epidermis","Primary lesions arise from infection while secondary lesions arise from an immune reaction to that infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Their combination determines the diagnostic category, also called the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman wants a mole on her back removed because her bra strap catches it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Removal must be preceded by a biopsy to confirm the lesion is not a melanoma","Removal is mandatory for any naevus subject to repeated friction from clothing","Removal for symptomatic or cosmetic reasons is reasonable, alongside sun protection counselling","Removal is not offered for symptoms, since a benign naevus should always be left alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and management is otherwise observation. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with a hobo spider bite asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesion will continue to advance daily until antiparasitic treatment is given","The wound heals within several weeks and headaches may last a week; treatment is supportive","The wound will need surgical reconstruction and healing will take several months","The symptoms will resolve within six hours with intravenous calcium gluconate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where is the eruption of dermatitis herpetiformis distributed?","choices":["On sun-exposed skin, sparing the chronically exposed face and hands","Symmetrically on the knees, elbows, buttocks and back","On the anterior shins bilaterally as tender nodules that do not ulcerate","On the face in the central third, sparing the nasolabial folds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — intensely pruritic papules and vesicles that group in a herpetiform pattern. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which examination priorities does the deck list for a suspected malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Good lighting and dermoscopy in every patient regardless of training; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa in every patient; a total-body survey reserved for those who already have a skin cancer diagnosis","Wood lamp examination and potassium hydroxide preparation of any scale; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when tinea is possible; regional nodes in every patient regardless of risk","Dermoscopy alone, since the total-body survey, the palms, soles and nails, the oral mucosa and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist rather than the referring clinician","Good lighting, dermoscopy when trained, and a total-body skin survey when indicated; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — note the oral mucosa is tied specifically to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a painful expanding leg ulcer and pyoderma gangrenosum is suspected. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Order an ankle-brachial index and venous duplex ultrasound only","Start empirical antibiotics and reassess the ulcer in two weeks","Biopsy the ulcer edge and take wound cultures, since the diagnosis is one of exclusion","Take a punch biopsy from the centre of the ulcer for direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — there is no gold standard test, so malignancy and infection must be excluded first. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"When the diagnosis carries some uncertainty, what balance must the decision to treat take into account?","choices":["The clinician's experience against published guidelines","The benefit of treating a sick person against the risk of erroneously treating a well person or one with a different disorder","The speed of the test against its accuracy","The benefit of screening the wider population against the financial cost of confirmatory testing for each positive result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's statement of the risk-versus-benefit balance under diagnostic uncertainty. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man who had photochemotherapy for psoriasis has brown-black irregular macules on his buttocks as well as his forearms. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Ephelides, which appear on sun-exposed skin in fair-skinned individuals","Naevus spilus, which is present at birth or within the first years of life","PUVA lentigines, which affect sun-protected as well as sun-exposed sites","Ordinary solar lentigines, which affect only chronically sun-exposed sites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they relate to the total number of treatments, male sex, fair skin and older age. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has an itchy, eczematous eruption on sun-exposed skin that extends onto covered areas, beginning after two weeks of a new sunscreen. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Minimal erythema dose testing to ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B","Skin biopsy of the eruption sent for direct immunofluorescence","Antinuclear antibody panel including anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies","Photopatch testing with duplicate sets, one of which is irradiated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is the gold standard for photoallergy; a reaction only on the irradiated set confirms it. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What does the lecture advise regarding where a patient sits on the continuum of behaviour change?","choices":["Refer every patient for specialist behavioural therapy","Apply the same standardised intervention regardless of where the patient sits on that continuum","Delay counselling until the patient is ready to change","Identify where they are and tailor interventions to their readiness and self-efficacy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture advises identifying the patient's position on the continuum and tailoring interventions to their readiness and self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with cutaneous larva migrans of the foot asks about treatment. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days, or ivermectin for one to two days","Cryotherapy applied to the advancing end of the track where the larva is located","Surgical excision of the leading edge of the lesion under local anaesthetic","Topical permethrin to the affected area with a second application at one week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is less effective and surgery or cryotherapy is not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is starting oral isotretinoin for nodular acne. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Pregnancy testing is required only before starting, after which monthly liver function tests replace it entirely","Contraception can be stopped as soon as the treatment course has been completed in full","Blood donation is encouraged during treatment because the drug is cleared through the plasma","Pregnancy testing is required before, monthly during and five weeks after treatment, with two forms of contraception preferred"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with iPledge enrolment, a one-month supply at a time, and no blood donation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with atopic dermatitis of the flexures develops weeping honey-coloured crusting over the affected eczema. Which best describes the infection?","choices":["A secondary bacterial infection of skin already damaged by another condition","A primary bacterial infection arising in previously normal intact skin","A primary fungal infection of skin already damaged by another condition","A foreign body reaction within skin already damaged by another condition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bullous impetigo can secondarily invade pre-existing lesions such as eczema. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is the immunological basis of dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against the epidermal basement membrane zone at the dermal junction","Immunoglobulin G antibodies against desmoglein between the keratinocytes","A T-cell mediated destruction of melanocytes within the affected epidermis","Immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase, forming complexes that deposit in the dermal papillae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — complement and neutrophils are then activated, producing subepidermal blisters. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When does herpes zoster scar?","choices":["Only in patients who go on to develop postherpetic neuralgia, whatever the depth of the lesions","Only when antiviral therapy is started later than 72 hours after the rash appears","When deeper epidermal or dermal layers are compromised by excoriation or secondary infection","Always, because the virus itself destroys the basement membrane wherever the lesions appear on the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it typically heals without visible sequelae otherwise. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 44-year-old bartender has had swollen, tender nail folds on several fingers for four months, with thickened discoloured nails and cuticles that have separated from the nail plate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic paronychia","Acute paronychia","Onychomycosis","Nail psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an inflammatory reaction to irritants over at least six weeks, with Candida albicans commonest. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has a brown band running the length of the left thumbnail. Over eight months it has widened, the lines within it vary in colour and spacing, and pigment now extends onto the skin of the proximal nail fold. Which finding is most concerning, and what is it called?","choices":["The variation in line colour and spacing — the Hutchinson sign, concerning for a benign melanonychia","The eight-month duration — the Hutchinson sign, concerning for a subungual haematoma","The pigment extending onto the proximal nail fold — the Hutchinson sign, highly concerning for nail unit melanoma","The widening of the band — the Hutchinson sign, highly concerning for nail unit melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it should prompt urgent expert evaluation regardless of other features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman noticed a single 3 cm oval pink patch on her back ten days ago; the centre has since cleared leaving a fine rim of scale. Now she has many smaller similar oval lesions across her trunk, their long axes running along the skin cleavage lines. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Pityriasis rosea","Guttate psoriasis","Tinea corporis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The herald patch with a collarette of scale, followed 1 to 2 weeks later by lesions in a Christmas tree pattern, is the picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"When should polymerase chain reaction be used in suspected herpes zoster, and from what?","choices":["Only in patients aged over 50 with typical disease; from paired serology rather than from the lesion itself","For atypical, disseminated, vaccine-modified or immunocompromised presentations; from vesicle fluid, scab, or cells from the lesion base","In every case before starting antivirals, whatever the presentation; from a blood sample rather than from the lesion or its vesicle fluid","Only after antiviral therapy has failed, and never at presentation; from a full-thickness skin biopsy rather than from vesicle fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — typical unilateral dermatomal vesicles are diagnosed clinically. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"How is dysplastic melanocytic naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Diagnosis is made clinically; management is observation alone with sun protection counselling, since these lesions never progress to melanoma at all","Diagnosis is clinical; management is cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal","Diagnosis is by Wood's lamp; management is phototherapy for extensive body surface involvement","Diagnosis is by biopsy; management is observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where melanoma is a concern, and sun protection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — melanoma risk rises with the number of naevi present. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Where is minoxidil primarily effective?","choices":["Over the entire scalp equally","Only in women","In the crown region of the scalp","At the frontal hairline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it prevents further loss and may produce regrowth, working best with finasteride. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has hot tub folliculitis over the trunk. He is systemically well and the rash began four days ago. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral ciprofloxacin for a ten-day course to eradicate the organism completely","Incision and drainage of the largest pustules with culture of the material","Topical mupirocin three times daily until every lesion has fully resolved","Reassurance with dilute acetic acid compresses, since most clear in two to ten days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 3 tablespoons of 5% vinegar in a pint of water, 20 minutes, two to four times daily. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"}]};
